   207→
   208→\textit{Context rot and long-context degradation:}
   209→Liu et al.~\cite{liu2024lost} showed that language models systematically miss information placed in the middle of a long input. Across question answering and key-value retrieval tasks, using both open-source (MPT-30B, LongChat) and closed models (GPT-3.5-Turbo, Claude), they found a U-shaped accuracy curve: retrieval is best when the relevant passage is near the start or end of the context and drops sharply when it is buried in the middle. This means that a larger context window does not automatically mean better results --- a finding that directly motivates Tokalator's per-file relevance scorer, which ranks files by predicted contribution rather than by recency alone.
   210→
   211→\textit{Context rot: evidence and mitigation:}
   212→Hong, Troynikov, and Huber~\cite{hong2025contextrot} introduced the term \emph{context rot} in an evaluation of 18~LLMs, showing that model performance drops unevenly as input length grows, even on simple tasks like needle retrieval and word repetition. They kept task difficulty constant and varied only input length, finding that distractor content makes things worse and that a well-structured haystack does not help retrieval accuracy. These results back Tokalator's context health warnings, which alert developers when their context size passes a provider-specific rot threshold and flag low-relevance tabs as likely distractors. Anthropic's applied AI team took the same view in their context engineering guide~\cite{anthropic2025contexteng}, treating context as a limited resource with diminishing returns because of the transformer's $n^2$ attention cost. They described three strategies for long-running agents: \emph{compaction} (summarizing and restarting the context window), \emph{structured note-taking} (keeping critical state outside the window), and \emph{sub-agent architectures} (handing focused sub-tasks to agents with clean windows). These ideas directly shaped Tokalator's \texttt{/compaction} command.
   213→
   214→\textit{Context compaction:}
   215→Navid~\cite{navid2025compaction} demonstrated automatic context compaction for tool-heavy agentic workflows, showing that a five-ticket customer service pipeline consuming 208\,K tokens without compaction could be reduced to 86\,K tokens (58.6\% reduction) with only two compaction events, while preserving task completion quality. The technique monitors token usage per turn, injects a summary prompt when a threshold is exceeded, and replaces the conversation history with the compressed summary. Navid's cookbook provided both SDK-based compaction via Anthropic's \texttt{compaction\_control} parameter and a manual chat-loop pattern applicable to any conversational application. This work directly inspired Tokalator's \texttt{/compaction} and \texttt{/preview} commands, which help developers anticipate when their context window will approach the rot threshold and plan conversation strategies accordingly. Notably, VS Code v1.110~\cite{vscode2026v110} subsequently introduced a native \texttt{/compact} command and automatic compaction for Copilot chat sessions, confirming the importance of this capability; Tokalator's approach differs in providing explicit threshold tracking, turn-by-turn growth projection, and cross-provider support rather than session-scoped automation within a single IDE.
   216→
   217→\textit{Agent context management:}
   218→Zhang et al.~\cite{zhang2025adaptive} built an adaptive context manager for QA agents that dynamically selects which history fragments to keep based on relevance to the current query --- the same idea Tokalator's tab relevance scorer applies to open IDE files. In code generation, Shi et al.~\cite{shi2024evor} introduced EVOR, which refines retrieved context documents during code synthesis; Tokalator solves the related problem of deciding which already-open files should stay in context. Zhang et al.~\cite{zhang2025ace} introduced Agentic Context Engineering (ACE), which treats the system prompt as a living document that grows richer through generation and reflection, gaining +10.6\% on agent benchmarks while cutting cost. ACE shows that actively managing what enters the context window produces better results than leaving it uncontrolled --- the same principle behind Tokalator's optimization-first design. Nanjundappa and Maaheshwari~\cite{nanjundappa2025branch} proposed ContextBranch, which applies version-control ideas (checkpoint, branch, switch, inject) to LLM conversations, cutting context size by 58.1\% in exploratory coding. ContextBranch and Tokalator target the same problem from different angles: Tokalator controls which files enter the context; ContextBranch controls which conversation turns stay.
   219→
   220→\textit{Cross-session and multi-agent context:}
   221→Vasilopoulos~\cite{vasilopoulos2026codified} described Codified Context, a three-part system (hot-memory constitution, 19 specialist agents, cold-memory knowledge base) used across 283 development sessions on a 108,000-line C\# codebase. It is the closest real-world counterpart to Tokalator: Tokalator manages the live context window within a session, while Codified Context organizes persistent cross-session memory to stop LLMs from forgetting project conventions. Together, the two cover the full lifecycle --- within a session (Tokalator) and across sessions (Codified Context). Zhu et al.~\cite{zhu2025onecontext} proposed OneContext, a self-managed context layer for multi-agent systems that records trajectories and supports shared checkpoints; it operates at the multi-agent level, while Tokalator focuses on the single-developer, single-agent context budget inside the IDE.
   222→
   223→\textit{Terminology:}
   224→The context engineering field still lacks agreed-on terms. For example, Anthropic calls the feature ``prompt caching,'' OpenAI calls it ``automatic caching,'' and Google calls it ``context caching'' --- three names for mechanically similar features with different pricing rules. Hong et al.~\cite{hong2025contextrot} coined ``context rot,'' while others use ``context degradation,'' ``attention dilution,'' or ``context pollution'' for overlapping ideas. Burnham and Adamczewski~\cite{burnham2025context} introduced ``context engineering'' itself, yet terms like ``compaction,'' ``distractors,'' and ``stable prefix'' remain informal. Tokalator contributes a dictionary of 41 terms across seven categories (context management, token economics, prompt engineering, memory strategies, caching, architecture, and evaluation) at \url{https://tokalator.wiki/dictionary}, and the extension's interface introduces operational terms such as \emph{budget level}, \emph{context health}, \emph{relevance score}, \emph{compaction point}, \emph{rot threshold}, and \emph{optimization score} that link abstract concepts to actionable indicators. 
