   511→
   512→The total estimated tokens are computed as the sum of five components:
   513→\begin{equation}
   514→  T_{\text{total}} = T_{\text{files}} + T_{\text{sys}} + T_{\text{instr}} + T_{\text{conv}} + T_{\text{out}}
   515→\end{equation}
   516→where $T_{\text{files}} = \sum_i \text{tokens}(\text{tab}_i)$ is the sum of per-file BPE token counts across all open tabs,
   517→$T_{\text{sys}} = 2{,}000$ is the estimated system prompt overhead (Copilot's hidden system prompt), $T_{\text{instr}} = 500 \times n_{\text{instr}}$ accounts for instruction files detected in the workspace, $T_{\text{conv}} = 800 \times t$ estimates accumulated conversation history at turn~$t$, and $T_{\text{out}} = 4{,}000$ reserves tokens for the model's response.
   518→These overhead constants are informed by empirical measurement of GitHub Copilot's context construction but are necessarily approximate since the actual assistant context logic is proprietary (see Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).
   519→
   520→\textit{2. Tab relevance scoring.}
   521→Each open tab receives a relevance score $R \in [0, 1]$ computed as a weighted sum of five signals:
   522→\begin{equation}
   523→\begin{aligned}
   524→R =\;& 0.25\,S_{\text{lang}} + 0.30\,S_{\text{import}} + 0.20\,S_{\text{path}} \\
   525→     &+ 0.15\,S_{\text{recency}} + 0.10\,S_{\text{diag}}
   526→\end{aligned}
   527→\end{equation} 
   528→where $S_{\text{lang}} \in \{0, 1\}$ indicates language match with the active file, $S_{\text{import}} \in \{0, 1\}$ indicates an import relationship (detected via regex patterns for TypeScript/JavaScript, Python, Go, Java, and a generic fallback), $S_{\text{path}} \in [0, 1]$ measures shared directory depth (computed as the ratio of shared path prefix segments to total depth), $S_{\text{recency}} \in \{0, 0.53, 1\}$ reflects edit recency (1.0 if edited within 2 minutes, 0.53 within 10 minutes, 0 otherwise), and $S_{\text{diag}} \in \{0, 1\}$ flags files with compiler diagnostics. Pinned and active files are overridden to $R = 1.0$.
   529→
   530→The weights were assigned based on the following design rationale:
   531→import relationships ($w=0.30$) receive the highest weight because a file explicitly imported by the active file is almost certainly needed by the coding assistant; language match ($w=0.25$) is the next strongest signal since cross-language files (e.g., \texttt{.json} configs open alongside \texttt{.tsx} code) are common distractors; path similarity ($w=0.20$) captures co-location patterns in typical project structures; edit recency ($w=0.15$) reflects the developer's current working set; and diagnostics ($w=0.10$) provide a weak signal that files with errors are being actively debugged.
   532→The $S_{\text{recency}}$ intermediate value of~0.53 corresponds to $\nicefrac{0.08}{0.15}$, the ratio of the partial recency credit (0.08 for files edited within 10 minutes) to the full recency weight.
   533→The default distractor threshold of $R < 0.3$ was chosen so that a file must match on at least two signals (e.g., same language + nearby path) to avoid being flagged.
   534→These weights are configurable and could benefit from empirical calibration in future work.
   535→Tabs scoring below the threshold are flagged as distractors.
   536→
   537→Algorithm~\ref{alg:scoring} formalizes the scoring and optimization procedure.
   538→
   539→\begin{algorithm}[htp!]
   540→\DontPrintSemicolon
   541→\SetAlgoLined
   542→\KwIn{Open tabs $\mathcal{T} = \{t_1, \dots, t_n\}$, active file $f$, threshold $\tau = 0.3$}
   543→\KwOut{Scored tabs with distractor labels; optimized tab set $\mathcal{T}'$}
   544→\BlankLine
   545→\ForEach{$t_i \in \mathcal{T}$}{
   546→  \If{$t_i$ is pinned \textbf{or} $t_i = f$}{
   547→    $R_i \gets 1.0$\;
   548→  }
   549→  \Else{
   550→    $S_{\text{lang}} \gets \mathbb{1}[\text{lang}(t_i) = \text{lang}(f)]$\;
   551→    $S_{\text{import}} \gets \mathbb{1}[\text{imports}(f) \ni t_i]$\;
   552→    $S_{\text{path}} \gets \text{sharedDepth}(t_i, f) \;/\; \text{totalDepth}(t_i)$\;
   553→    $S_{\text{recency}} \gets \begin{cases} 1.0 & \text{if edited} < 2\,\text{min ago} \\ 0.53 & \text{if edited} < 10\,\text{min ago} \\ 0 & \text{otherwise}\end{cases}$\;
   554→    $S_{\text{diag}} \gets \mathbb{1}[\text{diagnostics}(t_i) > 0]$\;
   555→    $R_i \gets 0.25 S_{\text{lang}} + 0.30 S_{\text{import}} + 0.20 S_{\text{path}} + 0.15 S_{\text{recency}} + 0.10 S_{\text{diag}}$\;
   556→  }
   557→  Label $t_i$ as \textit{distractor} if $R_i < \tau$\;
   558→}
   559→\BlankLine
   560→\tcp{Optimization: close distractors to free context budget}
   561→$\mathcal{D} \gets \{t_i \in \mathcal{T} \mid R_i < \tau\}$\;
   562→$\mathcal{T}' \gets \mathcal{T} \setminus \mathcal{D}$\;
   563→$\Delta T \gets \sum_{t_i \in \mathcal{D}} \text{tokens}(t_i)$\;
   564→\KwRet{$\mathcal{T}'$, scores $\{R_i\}$, freed tokens $\Delta T$}\;
   565→\caption{Tab Relevance Scoring and Context Optimization}
   566→\label{alg:scoring}
   567→\end{algorithm}
   568→
   569→\textit{3. Chat participant with 11 commands.}
   570→The \texttt{@tokalator} chat participant responds to: \texttt{/count} (budget status), \texttt{/breakdown} (per-file tokens), \texttt{/optimize} (close low-relevance tabs to free up context), \texttt{/pin}/\texttt{/unpin} (pin management), \texttt{/instructions} (scan instruction files and their token cost), \texttt{/model} (switch model profile), \texttt{/compaction} (per-turn growth analysis), \texttt{/preview} (preview next-turn token cost before sending), \texttt{/reset} and \texttt{/exit} (session management).
   571→
   572→\textit{4. Context optimization.}
   573→The \texttt{/optimize} command identifies open tabs with low relevance scores ($R < 0.3$) and closes them to free up context budget, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:chat}~(b). The dashboard also provides an \textit{Optimize Tabs} button that triggers the same behavior. This reduces attention dilution by removing files that are unlikely to be relevant to the current coding task.
   574→
   575→\textit{5. Session persistence.}
   576→Session summaries (peak tokens, turns, model, top edited files) are saved to \texttt{workspaceState} on exit and shown as a notification on the next activation.
   577→Pinned files and model selection persist across VS~Code restarts.
   578→
   579→\textit{6. Instruction file scanner.}
   580→The extension detects and tokenizes instruction files that AI coding assistants automatically inject into every prompt. It searches nine patterns covering all major coding assistant ecosystems: \texttt{.github/copilot-instructions.md}, \texttt{CLAUDE.md}, \texttt{AGENTS.md}, \texttt{.cursorrules}, \texttt{.instructions.md}, \texttt{.github/instructions/}, \texttt{.claude/*.md}, \texttt{.copilot/skills/}, and \texttt{.github/skills/}. This reveals the hidden token cost of instruction files that are otherwise invisible to the developer.
   581→
   582→\subsubsection{Web Platform}
   583→
   584→The web platform at \url{https://tokalator.wiki} includes nine calculators:
   585→
   586→\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
   587→  \item \textit{Cost Calculator}: Token cost calculation with Cobb--Douglas quality modeling for Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google models. Supports tiered pricing detection: when the total prompt length exceeds Anthropic's 200\,K-token threshold (available for Opus and Sonnet via the 1\,M context beta), \emph{all} input tokens in that request are billed at the extended rate (2$\times$ standard input cost), not only the tokens above the threshold. This is a binary tier switch, not a marginal rate, implemented via the \texttt{getPricingTier} function.
   588→
   589→  \item \textit{Context Optimizer}: Visualizes context window allocation across system prompt, user input, reserved output, and free space. Computes usage percentage and generates warnings.
   590→
   591→  \item \textit{Model Comparison}: Cross-provider cost and capability comparison across all 14 models.
   592→
   593→  \item \textit{Caching ROI Calculator}: Break-even analysis for prompt caching. Given $T$ tokens to cache with write cost~$c_w$ per token, read cost~$c_r$ per token, and standard input cost~$c_{\text{in}}$ per token, each reuse saves $(c_{\text{in}} - c_r)$ per token compared to re-sending the prompt uncached. The break-even reuse count is:
   594→  \begin{equation}
   595→    n^* = \left\lceil \frac{c_w}{c_{\text{in}} - c_r} \right\rceil
   596→  \end{equation}
   597→  For all current Anthropic models, $c_w = 1.25 \times c_{\text{in}}$ and $c_r = 0.10 \times c_{\text{in}}$, yielding $n^* = \lceil 1.25 / 0.90 \rceil = 2$ reuses. At 10 reuses the savings reach 76\%.
   598→
   599→  \item \textit{Conversation Estimator}: Multi-turn cost projection under three strategies, each defined by the input tokens sent at turn~$t$ (with system prompt~$S$, average user tokens~$u$, average assistant tokens~$a$):
   600→  \begin{itemize}
   601→    \item \textit{Full History}: $I_t = S + \sum_{i=1}^{t}(u_i + a_i)$, yielding $O(T^2)$ cumulative cost since $\sum_{t=1}^{T} I_t = ST + \tfrac{T(T+1)}{2}(u+a)$.
   602→    \item \textit{Sliding Window} ($W$ turns): \begin{equation*}
   603→    \begin{aligned}
   604→    I_t = S + \sum_{i=\max(1,\,t-W+1)}^{t} 
   605→          (u_i + a_i),
   606→    \end{aligned}
   607→    \end{equation*} capping per-turn cost at $S + W(u+a)$ for $t \geq W$, yielding $O(T)$ cumulative cost.
   608→    \item \textit{Summarize} (ratio $\rho$, keep last $k$ turns fresh): $I_t = S + \rho \cdot \sum_{i=1}^{t-k}(u_i + a_i) + \sum_{i=t-k+1}^{t}(u_i + a_i)$, growing slowly at rate $\rho(u+a)$ per turn.
   609→  \end{itemize}
   610→  Per-turn breakdown charts visualize the cost trajectories.
   611→
   612→  \item \textit{Economic Analysis}: Visualization of the Cobb--Douglas quality-of-output production function:
   613→  \begin{equation}
   614→    Q(X, Y, Z) = X^{\alpha} \cdot Y^{\beta} \cdot (b + Z)^{\gamma}
   615→  \end{equation}
   616→  where $X$ = input tokens, $Y$ = output tokens, $Z$ = cache/fine-tuning tokens, $b$ = base model quality, and $\alpha, \beta, \gamma$ are provider-specific sensitivity parameters ($\alpha + \beta + \gamma < 1$, ensuring diminishing returns to scale).
   617→
   618→  The corresponding cost minimization problem is:
   619→  \begin{equation}
   620→    \min_{X,Y,Z \geq 0} \; c_x X + c_y Y + c_z Z \qquad \text{s.t.} \quad Q(X,Y,Z) \geq \bar{Q}
   621→  \end{equation}
   622→  where $c_x, c_y, c_z$ are the per-token costs for input, output, and cache-write respectively.
   623→  Applying the Lagrangian and the first-order conditions (equating marginal-product-per-dollar ratios), the optimal allocation satisfies $X^*/Y^* = (\alpha\, c_y) / (\beta\, c_x)$, and the minimum cost for target quality~$\bar{Q}$ without caching is:
   624→  \begin{equation}
   625→    C^*(\bar{Q}) = (\alpha + \beta) \left(\frac{\bar{Q}}{b^{\gamma}}\right)^{\!1/(\alpha+\beta)} \left(\frac{c_x}{\alpha}\right)^{\!\alpha/(\alpha+\beta)} \left(\frac{c_y}{\beta}\right)^{\!\beta/(\alpha+\beta)}
   626→  \end{equation}
   627→  following Lemma~4 of Bergemann et al.~\cite{bergemann2025economics}. The with-caching variant includes $Z$ as a third decision variable, reducing cost when $c_z < c_x$.
   628→
   629→The sensitivity parameters ($\alpha = 0.30$, $\beta = 0.35$, $\gamma = 0.20$, $b = 1.0$ for Opus; $\alpha = 0.25$, $\beta = 0.30$, $\gamma = 0.20$, $b = 0.85$ for Sonnet; $\alpha = 0.20$, $\beta = 0.25$, $\gamma = 0.15$, $b = 0.70$ for Haiku) are author-assigned placeholder values that satisfy two structural constraints: (i)~$\alpha + \beta + \gamma < 1$ (diminishing returns to scale) and (ii)~$\alpha < \beta$ (output tokens matter more than input tokens for quality, reflecting the generation-quality intuition). No public benchmark measures output quality as a Cobb–Douglas function of token allocation, so the parameters cannot be calibrated from data at this time. Sensitivity analysis shows that the qualitative ranking of strategies (caching $>$ sliding window $>$ full history at high reuse rates) is robust across a $\pm 30\%$ perturbation of all parameters; the tool exposes sliders so users can explore parameter sensitivity interactively. Calibration from controlled output quality experiments is a priority for future work.
   630→
   631→  The tool renders quality isoquant curves and cost minimization surfaces with and without caching.
   632→
   633→  \item \textit{Usage Tracker}: Historical API usage analysis with cost breakdowns by model, project, and time period, plus linear regression and exponential smoothing projections.
   634→
   635→  \item \textit{Pricing Explorer}: Interactive cross-provider pricing comparison with bar charts and service-tier breakdowns. Displays per-million-token input, output, cache-write, and cache-read costs side by side for all 14~models across three providers, including Anthropic's extended-context pricing tiers.
   636→
   637→  \item \textit{Economics Explorer}: A live parameter-tuning dashboard that extends the Economic Analysis calculator with radar charts, multi-model overlays, and slider-driven ``what-if'' scenarios. Users can vary $\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$ and instantly see the effect on optimal allocations and minimum costs across providers.
   638→\end{enumerate}
   639→
   640→The platform also includes a 10-lesson context engineering course (\url{https://tokalator.wiki/learn}) that progresses from basic tokenization (``What Is a Prompt'') through intermediate concepts (trimming, summarisation, context management, context pollution) to production patterns (automatic compaction via Anthropic's \texttt{compaction\_control} API, context editing with memory tools, and a full customer-service pipeline walkthrough). A wiki (\url{https://tokalator.wiki/wiki}) aggregates articles from arXiv, OpenAI Cookbook, Anthropic documentation, and Google AI docs through an automated twice-monthly fetch pipeline defined in \texttt{wiki-sources.json}. A dictionary of 41 context engineering terms organized across seven categories is available at \url{https://tokalator.wiki/dictionary}.
   641→
   642→\subsubsection{Context Engineering Catalog}
   643→
   644→The catalog auto-discovers reusable artifacts from the repository using file extension conventions: \texttt{.agent.md} (agents), \texttt{.prompt.md} (prompts), \texttt{.instructions.md} (workspace guidelines), \texttt{.collection.yml} (bundles), and \texttt{CLAUDE.md} (Claude Code instructions).
   645→A \texttt{user-content/} directory accepts community contributions, which are automatically indexed with YAML frontmatter parsing.
   646→A \texttt{catalog-config.json} file specifies scan directories and featured artifact IDs for the landing page.
   647→The web platform provides dedicated browsing pages for each artifact type (\texttt{/agents}, \texttt{/collections}, \texttt{/prompts}, \texttt{/instructions}) with dynamic detail routes (e.g., \texttt{/agents/[id]}), plus a \texttt{/context-engineering} overview page that lists all available artifacts with their purposes.
   648→
   649→\subsection{Computational complexity}
   650→\label{sec:complexity}
   651→
   652→The VS~Code extension's performance budget is determined by the interactive loop: every user event (tab switch, file edit, diagnostic change) triggers a snapshot rebuild whose latency must remain imperceptible ($<$\,16\,ms to avoid dropped frames at 60\,fps).
   653→
   654→\textit{Snapshot build.} Let $n$ be the number of open tabs. Building a snapshot iterates over all tabs once to collect cached token counts, yielding $O(n)$ per refresh. Token counts are cached per document, keyed by URI and version; re-tokenization occurs only when a document's version changes, making the amortized per-edit cost $O(L)$ where $L$ is the length of the changed document. The BPE tokenizers (Claude~696\,KB JSON, o200k\_base via \texttt{js-tiktoken}) are lazy-loaded once, adding ${\sim}100$--$200$\,ms on first activation, but $O(1)$ thereafter.
   655→
   656→\textit{Relevance scoring.} Algorithm~\ref{alg:scoring} scores each tab in $O(n)$: language match and diagnostics are $O(1)$ lookups; import detection parses the active file's imports once ($O(I)$, where $I$ is the number of import lines) and checks membership for each tab via a hash set, yielding $O(n + I)$ total; path similarity is $O(d)$ per tab where $d$ is the directory depth, bounded by a small constant in practice. The overall scoring pass is $O(n \cdot d + I)$, which in practice completes in $<$\,5\,ms for 30+~open tabs.
   657→
   658→\textit{Space complexity.} The extension maintains one \texttt{ContextSnapshot} object in memory, holding per-tab metadata (URI, token count, relevance score, language ID): $O(n)$ total. No history of snapshots is retained; session summaries are flushed to \texttt{workspaceState} as a single JSON string of bounded size.
   659→
   660→\textit{Web platform calculators.} All nine calculators evaluate closed-form expressions (Equations~3--7) in $O(1)$ for a given parameter set. The Conversation Estimator computes cumulative cost over $T$~turns, yielding $O(T)$ for the full-history strategy and $O(T)$ for sliding-window (with a constant window cap). Chart rendering is $O(T)$ points. No optimization loop or iterative solver is required.
   661→
   662→\textit{Event throttling.} A 300\,ms debounce timer bounds refresh frequency to at most $\sim$3~rebuilds per second regardless of event rate, preventing UI blocking during rapid editing.
   663→
   664→
   665→\section{Illustrative examples}
   666→\label{sec:examples}
   667→
   668→\textit{Example 1: Identifying context budget waste in a VS~Code session:} A developer working on a React project has 23 tabs open.
   669→After installing Tokalator, the status bar shows: \texttt{\$(warning) 85.2K / 200K (42.6\%) -- Claude Opus 4.6}. The developer types \texttt{@tokalator /breakdown} in the chat panel and sees that 12 tabs are configuration files (\texttt{.json}, \texttt{.yml}) contributing 18\,K tokens but scoring below the 0.3 relevance threshold. Running \texttt{@tokalator /optimize} closes these 12 tabs, reducing context usage to 67.2K tokens (33.6\%). The developer then runs \texttt{@tokalator /instructions} and discovers that their \texttt{.github/copilot-instructions.md} file alone costs 4,200 tokens, injected silently into every prompt.

