%% ── Abstract (JSS limit: 250 words) ─────────────────────────────────
%% Word count verified: 248 words
\begin{abstract}
AI coding assistants operate within finite context windows of 128K to
1M tokens.  When developers accumulate open files, instruction files,
and long conversation histories, model attention degrades and API costs
grow quadratically---yet no cross-provider cost-modelling tool
previously surfaced these costs inside the IDE.  We present Tokalator,
an open-source context engineering toolkit with six components:
a VS~Code extension monitoring token budgets in real time, scoring open
tabs by syntactic relevance across five signals, and closing
\emph{distractor tabs} (files with predicted relevance below a
configurable threshold) via 11 chat commands;
a web platform with nine interactive calculators implementing
closed-form caching break-even analysis ($n^*=2$ for Anthropic models),
Cobb--Douglas quality optimisation, and three conversation cost
strategies with proven $O(T^2)$ growth;
a catalog of 146+ agents, 140+ prompts, and 161+ instruction files;
a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server and CLI for Claude Code
integration; a Python API for econometric computation; and a PostgreSQL
database for historical usage analytics.
The system covers 17 models from three providers (Anthropic, OpenAI,
Google) and is validated by 124 unit tests.
A structured survey of 50 software engineers and presentations to over
220 developers across three venues confirm that instruction files and
low-relevance configuration tabs are the primary invisible budget
consumers.
Tokalator reduced context usage by 21.2\% in a representative session;
prompt caching analysis showed 88.9\% daily savings for a 50K-token
system prompt at 100 daily reuses.
The VS~Code extension achieved a 77.62\% page-to-acquisition conversion
rate within its first 30 days on the Marketplace.
\end{abstract}

