   106→\section{Introduction and Motivation}
   107→
   108→Modern AI coding assistants such as GitHub Copilot, Claude Code, Cursor, and other integrated development environments (IDEs) serve as software applications that help programmers develop code efficiently. The ``State of AI'' empirical study~\cite{aubakirova2025state}, based on more than 100 trillion tokens of real-world interactions on the OpenRouter platform, reveals a significant shift in how large LLMs are used. Consumption is increasingly dominated by agentic workflows, technical tasks, and reasoning-intensive models. The average number of prompt tokens per request increased nearly fourfold (from about 1,500 to over 6,000) between early 2024 and late 2025. Completion tokens nearly tripled (from about 150 to 400), resulting in a total average sequence length of over 5,400 tokens by late 2025. The IDEs are now powered by LLM providers with context sizes ranging from 128,000 to 1,000,000 tokens (as of February 2026). Despite these large context windows, developers lack visibility into how their context budget is consumed. Every open tab, system prompt, instruction file, and conversation turn contributes to this budget. For example, with Anthropic's Claude Opus 4.6 pricing at \$5.00 per million input tokens and \$25.00 per million output tokens~\cite{anthropic2026pricing}, a single 200,000-token prompt costs \$1.00 for input alone.
   109→
   110→The rapid growth of agentic coding tools (GitHub Copilot, Claude Code, Cursor) makes this worse: Robbes et al.~\cite{robbes2026agentic} found 15.85--22.60\% adoption of coding agents across 129,134 GitHub projects, with agentic sessions consuming far more tokens per interaction than traditional completions. Han et al.~\cite{han2024talebudget} showed that token budgets can be enforced at the reasoning level, compressing chain-of-thought steps without significant quality loss, but no IDE tool previously exposed this budget control in a cross-provider, cost-modeling form (VS~Code~v1.110, released concurrently~\cite{vscode2026v110}, introduced a native context indicator scoped to Copilot sessions only).
   111→
   112→Existing tools cover only parts of this problem: \texttt{tiktoken}~\cite{sennrich2016bpe} and Anthropic's tokenizer count tokens offline but have no IDE integration, no cross-provider support, and no cost modeling. Bergemann et al.~\cite{bergemann2025economics} built a formal economic framework for LLM output quality but did not turn it into a developer tool. Without integrated tooling, three problems arise:
   113→
   114→\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
   115→  \item \textit{Attention dilution}: irrelevant files compete with relevant ones for the context window, reducing model output quality~\cite{bergemann2025economics,hong2025contextrot}.
   116→  \item \textit{Cost escalation}: conversations that send full history at every turn grow at $O(T^2)$ cumulative cost, because at turn~$t$ the model receives $S + t(u + a)$ input tokens (system prompt~$S$, average user tokens~$u$, average assistant tokens~$a$), so total input cost is $\sum_{t=1}^{T}[S + t(u+a)] = ST + \tfrac{T(T+1)}{2}(u+a) \in O(T^2)$.
   117→  \item \textit{Context rot}: after 20+ turns, stale context hurts model accuracy; Hong et al.~\cite{hong2025contextrot} showed this degradation is uneven across models, appears even on simple retrieval tasks, and gets worse when distractor content is present.
   118→\end{enumerate}
   119→
   120→This paper addresses three research questions:\label{sec:intro_rqs}
   121→
   122→\begin{description}
   123→  \item[\textbf{RQ1}] \textit{What are the primary sources of token budget consumption in a typical AI-assisted development session, and can they be made visible to developers in real time?}
   124→  \item[\textbf{RQ2}] \textit{Can a lightweight syntactic relevance scorer reliably identify distractor tabs that developers agree are not relevant to the current task?}
   125→  \item[\textbf{RQ3}] \textit{Can formal economic models (Cobb--Douglas production functions, caching break-even analysis, conversation cost projections) be turned into practical developer tools without needing access to proprietary model internals?}
   126→\end{description}
   127→
   128→We present Tokalator, an intelligent developer assistance system that observes IDE state, reasons about context budget health, and recommends or takes corrective actions --- the core loop of an expert system applied to a software engineering task. Its relevance scorer acts as a knowledge-based inference engine (applying weighted rules over five observable signals), and its economic calculators form a decision support system that translates formal LLM cost models into actionable recommendations without requiring developers to work through the underlying mathematics. Tokalator makes the following contributions:
   129→\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
   130→  \item A \textit{real-time context budget monitor} integrated into VS~Code that decomposes token consumption across five categories (files, system prompt, instructions, conversation, output reservation) and raises health warnings based on provider-specific rot thresholds.
   131→  \item A \textit{five-signal tab relevance scorer} that ranks open files by predicted contribution to the AI response and closes distractors on demand, reducing context usage by approximately 21\% in our illustrative example (Section~\ref{sec:examples}); broader deployment savings vary by project and tab management habits.
   132→  \item \textit{Closed-form economic models} for caching break-even ($n^* = \lceil c_w / (c_\mathrm{in} - c_r) \rceil = 2$ for Anthropic models), conversation cost projection under three strategies, and Cobb–Douglas quality optimization, packaged in interactive web calculators.
   133→  \item A \textit{Model Context Protocol (MCP) server and CLI} that brings the same BPE token counting directly into Claude Code and any MCP-capable agent.
   134→  \item A \textit{context engineering catalog} with an open contribution structure: reusable agents, prompts, instruction files, and collections are auto-discovered from community-contributed directories using file-extension conventions; an initial seed of curated artifacts is included and the catalog grows with community submissions.
   135→\end{enumerate}
