\textit{1-) Tokenization libraries:} OpenAI's \texttt{tiktoken} and Anthropic's \texttt{claude-tokenizer} provide programmatic token counting for their respective BPE vocabularies~\cite{sennrich2016bpe,openai2025cookbook}. Each provider uses a distinct encoding: OpenAI models use \texttt{cl100k\_base} or \texttt{o200k\_base}, while Anthropic models use their proprietary BPE vocabulary. Anthropic exposes a server-side token counting endpoint via the Messages \ac{API}~\cite{anthropic2025tokencounting}; Tokalator replicates this client-side via \texttt{@anthropic-ai / tokenizer}, enabling offline, zero-\ac{API}-call token counts. Neither library offers real-time IDE integration, multi-provider comparison, or cost estimation.\kXX{Since we claim multi-provider support (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google) in Table 2, we must briefly acknowledge here how we handle Google models. State that since Google lacks a client-side BPE tokenizer, Tokalator uses a character heuristic. Mention the measured MAE error bounds (10-15\%) here. Don't hide the limitation until Section 6; own it upfront to build engineering trust.}

\textit{2-) LLM pricing and inference economics:} Bergemann, Bonatti, and Smolin~\cite{bergemann2025economics} formalized LLM output quality as a Cobb–Douglas production function of input, output, and cached tokens, but did not provide an implementation. Erdil~\cite{erdil2025pareto} analyzed Pareto frontiers of inference cost versus capability, while Cottier et al.~\cite{cottier2025price} documented rapid but uneven price declines across tasks. Yotta Labs~\cite{yotta2026econ} examined inference economics beyond per-token pricing. These studies provide economic theory but no developer-facing tools.\kXX{This is the perfect place to set up our defense of the Cobb-Douglas parameters. Add a sentence noting that translating this theory into a developer tool requires estimating the $\alpha, \beta, \gamma$ sensitivity parameters, and state that our implementation demonstrates economic robustness even under $\pm 30\%$ parameter perturbations. This preempts reviewer complaints about construct validity.}

\textit{3-) Context window research:}
The ``State of AI'' study by Aubakirova et al.~\cite{aubakirova2025state} documented approximately a 4$\times$ increase in average prompt length on OpenRouter (from 1.5\,K to 6\,K tokens) between 2024 and 2025, driven by agentic workflows. Emberson et al.~\cite{emberson2025length} showed that LLM responses have been growing longer over time, further increasing context pressure. Fu et al.~\cite{fu2024scaling} demonstrated that data engineering choices (mixture ratios and sequence packing) critically determine whether models can exploit 128K context windows at all, reinforcing that context length is a resource to be managed, not merely maximized. Wei et al.~\cite{wei2025tokenweighting} proposed position-aware token weighting, showing that not all tokens in the context window contribute equally to generation quality, a finding that theoretically supports Tokalator's approach of scoring tabs by predicted contribution rather than treating all open files as equally valuable. Burnham and Adamczewski~\cite{burnham2025context} introduced the term ``context engineering'' to describe the systematic design of what enters the context window.

\textit{4-) Context rot and long-context degradation:}
Hong et al.~\cite{hong2025contextrot} introduced the term \emph{context rot} in a systematic evaluation of 18~LLMs, demonstrating that model performance degrades non-uniformly as input length increases, even on simple tasks such as non-lexical needle retrieval and word repetition. Their experiments isolated input length as the primary factor by holding task complexity constant, revealing that distractors amplify degradation and that structural coherence of the haystack hurts rather than helps retrieval accuracy. These findings provide empirical grounding for Tokalator's context health analyzers, which warn developers when context size exceeds provider-specific rot thresholds and flag low-relevance tabs as potential distractors \kXX{This is a nice theoretical connection, but we need empirical backing. If we have a labeled set of human judgments, cite it here and point to the quantitative evaluation. Otherwise, avoid implying that the $R<0.3$ threshold is validated, and present it as a conservative heuristic threshold chosen for practical use.}. Anthropic's applied AI team formalized this perspective in their context engineering guide~\cite{anthropic2025contexteng}, arguing that context must be treated as a finite resource with diminishing marginal returns due to the transformer's $n^2$ pairwise attention relationships. They introduced three complementary strategies for long-horizon agents, \emph{compaction} (summarizing and reinitializing context windows), \emph{structured note-taking} (persisting critical state outside the window), and \emph{sub-agent architectures} (delegating focused tasks to agents with clean windows), which directly inform Tokalator's \texttt{/compaction} command that tracks per-turn growth against the rot threshold.
