   803→\subsection{Community Survey: Qualitative Feedback ($n = 50$)}
   804→\label{sec:survey}
   805→
   806→We conducted a structured 10-item survey with 50 software engineers from
   807→\ifanonymous
   808→three developer communities (names withheld for review).
   809→\else
   810→the iLab/Kariyer.net, TechCareer, and SaaS Bridge communities.
   811→\fi
   812→Data were collected in person during the three community sessions and in a follow-up online session for remote participants, using structured note-taking and consolidated into an Excel file; no third-party survey platform was used. Participants used the extension for at least one working day before responding. Responses were coded inductively; the survey was not pre-registered and participants self-selected, so findings are treated as qualitative and hypothesis-generating rather than confirmatory. Full theme descriptions, Likert distributions, demographics, and verbatim quotes are in Appendix~\ref{app:survey}.
   813→
   814→Five themes emerged. The \texttt{/preview} command was the most-valued feature (82\% of respondents), with participants discovering that a single turn can cost 6K+ tokens and adjusting their prompts after seeing the breakdown. Tokenizer accuracy was a concern for non-English users: developers writing Turkish or Arabic code found the Google heuristic ($\approx$4 chars/token) underestimated counts by 15--32\%. Model synchronization was the most-requested fix: 64\% of respondents noticed their Copilot chat model and Tokalator's status bar were out of sync, which v3.1.2 resolved by auto-reading \texttt{request.model} on every chat command. On session management, 38\% asked for persistent cross-session history, and several reported the pin/unpin bug now fixed in v3.1.2. Finally, 48\% of respondents used \texttt{/compaction}, with 67\% of that group saying it helped them spot the compaction point before hitting the context limit; the main gap was that the output could not yet separate file tokens from conversation tokens.
   815→
   816→\subsection{Controlled Deployment Experiment (In Progress)}
   817→\label{sec:experiment}
   818→
   819→Based on the qualitative findings from the survey and deployment, we designed a within-subjects crossover experiment to measure whether context budget visibility \textit{quantitatively} reduces context consumption in real coding sessions.
   820→
   821→\textit{Design.} Each of $N$ participants ($N = 20$--30, recruited from
   822→\ifanonymous
   823→the same developer population as the deployment study)
   824→\else
   825→the iLab/Kariyer.net engineering team)
   826→\fi
   827→completes two 1-hour coding sessions on real work tasks: one with Tokalator \textbf{disabled} (control) and one with Tokalator \textbf{enabled} (treatment). Assignment order is counterbalanced (half start with control, half with treatment) with a minimum 1-day washout between sessions.
   828→
   829→\textit{Primary outcome.} Total estimated context tokens at session end (open tabs $+$ overhead), captured automatically by the extension's session logger in the treatment condition and by a single end-of-session snapshot in the control condition.
   830→
   831→\textit{Secondary outcomes.} Number of open tabs, distractor ratio ($R < 0.3$ / total tabs), \texttt{/optimize} invocations (treatment only), and three post-session Likert items (5-point scale) measuring budget awareness, perceived response relevance, and tool adoption intent.
   832→
   833→\textit{Session logging.} The opt-in \texttt{SessionLogger} module (enabled via \texttt{tokalator.enableSessionLogging}) records only aggregate metrics: tab count, total tokens, budget utilisation percentage, per-tab relevance scores (by language ID, no file names), and command names. No personally identifiable information, file names, or file contents are captured.
   834→
   835→\textit{Analysis plan.} Paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests compare treatment vs.\ control on the primary and secondary outcomes. Effect sizes are reported as Cohen's $d$ with 95\% confidence intervals. Subjective Likert scores are compared with paired Wilcoxon tests. The full protocol, survey instrument, and analysis scripts are included in the repository (\texttt{evaluation/experiment-protocol.md}, \texttt{scripts/analyze-experiment.py}).
   836→
   837→\textit{Status.} The experiment infrastructure (session logger, snapshot collector, analysis scripts) is implemented and tested. Participant recruitment and data collection are underway at the time of submission; results will be incorporated into the final manuscript.
