   956→\section{Discussion}
   957→\label{sec:discussion}
   958→
   959→\subsection{Interpretation of Results}
   960→
   961→The three research questions share a common finding: token budget consumption in AI-assisted development is large, invisible by default, and highly manageable once made visible. The 21.2\% context reduction in Example~1 came entirely from removing files the developer had not intentionally included as context --- they were simply open tabs accumulated across a working session. Similarly, the 88.9\% daily cost saving in Example~2 was available to any team using a 50K-token system prompt, but only became actionable when the break-even calculation was presented concretely. These results suggest that the primary barrier to context efficiency is not developer motivation but information availability.
   962→
   963→The survey results reinforce this: 82\% of respondents named \texttt{/preview} as their most-valued feature, not because it changes what the model sees, but because it makes the cost of the next turn observable before it is incurred. This aligns with the broader literature on decision support systems: making a cost function explicit and real-time tends to shift behavior even without enforcement \citep{bergemann2025economics}.
   964→
   965→The model synchronization issue (64\% of users affected) highlights a structural gap in how current AI coding tools expose their state: developers switch models in the Copilot UI without any notification to companion tools. Tokalator's v3.1.2 auto-sync addresses this at the extension level, but a standard API for broadcasting active model changes across IDE extensions would benefit the entire ecosystem.
   966→
   967→\subsection{Practical Implications}
   968→
   969→For development teams adopting AI coding assistants, the findings from this work suggest four actionable guidelines:
   970→
   971→\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
   972→  \item \textit{Audit instruction files first.} A \texttt{CLAUDE.md} or \texttt{.github/copilot-instructions.md} file is injected silently into every prompt. A 4,000-token instruction file costs the same as 4,000 tokens of source code --- but applies to every single request. Teams should treat instruction files as high-ROI targets for context reduction.
   973→  \item \textit{Enable prompt caching for any system prompt used more than twice per day.} The break-even point for Anthropic models is two reuses. For OpenAI's automatic caching, the mechanism is always-on. Any team running a chatbot or agent with a fixed system prompt is losing money by not caching.
   974→  \item \textit{Use sliding-window or summarize strategies for conversations longer than 20 turns.} Full-history cost grows quadratically; switching to a 5-turn sliding window triples the number of affordable turns within the same daily budget (28 vs.\ 83 turns in Example~3).
   975→  \item \textit{Close distractor tabs before starting a coding session.} Configuration files (\texttt{.json}, \texttt{.yml}) open alongside active source files contribute tokens without providing useful context for most coding tasks. Running \texttt{@tokalator /optimize} at session start is a low-effort habit that reduces per-request cost immediately.
   976→\end{enumerate}
   977→
   978→\subsection{Threats to Validity}
   979→
   980→\textit{Internal validity.} The relevance score weights ($w_\mathrm{lang}=0.25$, $w_\mathrm{import}=0.30$, $w_\mathrm{path}=0.20$, $w_\mathrm{recency}=0.15$, $w_\mathrm{diag}=0.10$) were set by design reasoning, not measured against data. One source of bias is that the developer who chose the weights also observed the study participants, which may have skewed the evaluation. Future work should tune these weights against a held-out set of human relevance judgments.
   981→
   982→\textit{Construct validity.} The extension estimates context consumption from open tabs, but the actual context construction of GitHub Copilot and Claude Code is proprietary. The extension's token count is an upper-bound proxy, not a direct measurement. All reported figures (e.g., ``21\% reduction'') reflect estimated consumption, not confirmed API-level context size.
   983→
   984→\textit{External validity.} The user study ran at three venues:
   985→\ifanonymous
   986→an internal corporate session ($n \approx 50$), and two external community sessions ($n \approx 90$ and $n \approx 80$),
   987→\else
   988→Kariyer.net internal ($n \approx 50$), SaaS Bridge ($n \approx 90$), and TechCareer ($n \approx 80$),
   989→\fi
   990→totalling $N > 220$ developers. The internal group shared a similar TypeScript/React stack; the community sessions had a wider mix of languages and roles. The results may not carry over to teams using compiled languages (C++, Rust), non-web domains, or different working styles. The sample is large enough to identify recurring feedback themes but not for statistical generalization.
   991→
   992→\textit{Reliability.} The Cobb--Douglas sensitivity parameters ($\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$) are placeholder values satisfying structural constraints ($\alpha + \beta + \gamma < 1$) but not calibrated from empirical data. Users relying on the Economic Analysis tool for investment decisions should treat its outputs as directional guidance, not precise predictions.
   993→
   994→\subsection{Methodological Assumptions}
   995→
   996→The system design rests on several assumptions that should be made explicit.
   997→
   998→\textit{(A1) Open tabs approximate context.} Tokalator counts tokens from open tabs and adds fixed overheads for system prompts, instructions, and conversation history. The actual context construction of GitHub Copilot, Claude Code, and Cursor is proprietary; these tools may include additional signals (git diffs, terminal output, recently visited files) or exclude files the extension counts as open. The estimates are therefore upper-bound approximations, not exact context measurements.
