The table shows that the primary benefit of Tokalator is not that it changes the underlying model behavior, but that it makes invisible costs visible and helps developers choose better strategies. Context reduction of 21.2\% represents tokens that were being sent to the model unnecessarily in the unassisted workflow. The 88.9\% cost saving from prompt caching is a known-achievable optimization that developers without a calculator consistently overlooked in our survey (only 12\% of respondents had calculated their caching break-even before using Tokalator).

\subsection{Community Survey: Qualitative Feedback ($n = 50$)}
\label{sec:survey}

We conducted a structured 10-item survey with 50 software engineers from
\ifanonymous
three developer communities (names withheld for review).
\else
the iLab/Kariyer.net, TechCareer, and SaaS Bridge communities.
\fi
Data were collected in person during the three community sessions and in a follow-up online session for remote participants, using structured note-taking and consolidated into an Excel file; no third-party survey platform was used. Participants used the extension for at least one working day before responding. Responses were coded inductively; the survey was not pre-registered and participants self-selected, so findings are treated as qualitative and hypothesis-generating rather than confirmatory. Full theme descriptions, Likert distributions, demographics, and verbatim quotes are in Appendix~\ref{app:survey}.

Five themes emerged. The \texttt{/preview} command was the most-valued feature (82\% of respondents), with participants discovering that a single turn can cost 6K+ tokens and adjusting their prompts after seeing the breakdown. Tokenizer accuracy was a concern for non-English users: developers writing Turkish or Arabic code found the Google heuristic ($\approx$4 chars/token) underestimated counts by 15--32\%. Model synchronization was the most-requested fix: 64\% of respondents noticed their Copilot chat model and Tokalator's status bar were out of sync, which v3.1.2 resolved by auto-reading \texttt{request.model} on every chat command. On session management, 38\% asked for persistent cross-session history, and several reported the pin/unpin bug now fixed in v3.1.2. Finally, 48\% of respondents used \texttt{/compaction}, with 67\% of that group saying it helped them spot the compaction point before hitting the context limit; the main gap was that the output could not yet separate file tokens from conversation tokens.

\subsection{Controlled Deployment Experiment (In Progress)}
\label{sec:experiment}

Based on the qualitative findings from the survey and deployment, we designed a within-subjects crossover experiment to measure whether context budget visibility \textit{quantitatively} reduces context consumption in real coding sessions.

\textit{Design.} Each of $N$ participants ($N = 20$--30, recruited from
\ifanonymous
the same developer population as the deployment study)
\else
the iLab/Kariyer.net engineering team)
\fi
completes two 1-hour coding sessions on real work tasks: one with Tokalator \textbf{disabled} (control) and one with Tokalator \textbf{enabled} (treatment). Assignment order is counterbalanced (half start with control, half with treatment) with a minimum 1-day washout between sessions.

\textit{Primary outcome.} Total estimated context tokens at session end (open tabs $+$ overhead), captured automatically by the extension's session logger in the treatment condition and by a single end-of-session snapshot in the control condition.

\textit{Secondary outcomes.} Number of open tabs, distractor ratio ($R < 0.3$ / total tabs), \texttt{/optimize} invocations (treatment only), and three post-session Likert items (5-point scale) measuring budget awareness, perceived response relevance, and tool adoption intent.

\textit{Session logging.} The opt-in \texttt{SessionLogger} module (enabled via \texttt{tokalator.enableSessionLogging}) records only aggregate metrics: tab count, total tokens, budget utilisation percentage, per-tab relevance scores (by language ID, no file names), and command names. No personally identifiable information, file names, or file contents are captured.

\textit{Analysis plan.} Paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests compare treatment vs.\ control on the primary and secondary outcomes. Effect sizes are reported as Cohen's $d$ with 95\% confidence intervals. Subjective Likert scores are compared with paired Wilcoxon tests. The full protocol, survey instrument, and analysis scripts are included in the repository (\texttt{evaluation/experiment-protocol.md}, \texttt{scripts/analyze-experiment.py}).

\textit{Status.} The experiment infrastructure (session logger, snapshot collector, analysis scripts) is implemented and tested. Participant recruitment and data collection are underway at the time of submission; results will be incorporated into the final manuscript.

\subsection{Real-World AI Development Cost Analysis}
\label{sec:cost}

To complement the qualitative and quantitative evaluations above, this section presents the actual GitHub Copilot billing data recorded during Tokalator's 30-day development sprint (February~6 -- March~7, 2026). The data provides a rare empirical trace of AI-assisted software engineering costs at the individual-developer level, covering 1,413 premium AI requests across three model families --- Claude Opus~4.6, GPT~5.3, and GPT Codex~5.4 --- used via the GitHub Copilot interface. All data were exported from the GitHub Copilot usage dashboard and reflect the \texttt{copilot\_premium\_request} SKU at the published list price of \$0.04 per request.

Table~\ref{tab:billing-summary} summarises the key cost metrics. Total gross cost for the 1,413 premium requests was \$56.52. GitHub applied \$28.16 in billing credits (49.8\% effective discount), yielding a total net developer cost of \$28.36 --- less than \$30 for a full production-quality, 20,814-LOC, multi-component toolkit. GitHub Actions CI/CD consumed 261~minutes across 27 workflow runs (ci.yml, CodeQL security scanning, Dependabot updates, and wiki-fetch scheduling); all Actions usage fell within the free tier, contributing \$0 to net cost. The average net AI cost on active development days was \$1.49/day, peaking at \$8.20 on February~11 (the highest-cost session).

