  1485→\section{Survey Instrument and Aggregated Results}
  1486→\label{app:survey}
  1487→
  1488→This appendix provides the full 10-item survey instrument used in Section~\ref{sec:survey}, the participant demographic profile (including gender distribution: 35~Male, 13~Female, 2~Non-binary or undisclosed), aggregated Likert-scale response distributions with bar charts, the five-theme qualitative summary, and representative verbatim quotes.
  1489→
  1490→\subsection*{A.5.0 \quad Qualitative Theme Descriptions}
  1491→
  1492→\textit{Theme 1 -- Preview and Turn Improvement.}
  1493→Forty-one of 50 respondents (82\%) named the \texttt{/preview} command as the most immediately useful feature. Representative quotes: ``I never realized a single turn could cost 6K tokens; the preview showed me my system prompt was the main culprit'' and ``I cut my average turn from 4,200 to 1,800 tokens after seeing the per-turn breakdown.'' Participants who ran \texttt{/preview} before sending proactively trimmed their context; those who skipped it could not estimate per-turn cost. Eleven participants (22\%) asked for a \emph{suggested turn structure} that would split large messages into smaller, cheaper exchanges --- noted as future work.
  1494→
  1495→\textit{Theme 2 -- Tokenizer Accuracy.}
  1496→Twenty-six respondents (52\%) raised tokenizer accuracy concerns, mostly around non-English content. Eight developers working on Turkish or Arabic codebases found the Google heuristic ($\approx$4 chars/token) underestimated counts by 15--32\% compared to Google's \texttt{countTokens} API. Three respondents flagged that the \texttt{cl100k\_base} vocabulary was used for OpenAI models that had moved to \texttt{o200k\_base}; this was confirmed and corrected in v3.1.2. Five participants asked how Tokalator would handle future BPE vocabularies --- addressed in the Limitations section.
  1497→
  1498→\textit{Theme 3 -- Model Synchronization.}
  1499→Thirty-two respondents (64\%) encountered the model-mismatch problem: their Copilot chat had switched to Claude Opus~4.6 while Tokalator's status bar still showed GPT~5.3 from a previous session, producing wrong budget figures. This was the most-requested fix and drove the auto-sync feature in v3.1.2, which reads \texttt{request.model} on every \texttt{@tokalator} command. After the fix was explained, 29 of the 32 (91\%) said it met their need; 3 asked for the sync to also update the web calculator automatically (planned).
  1500→
  1501→\textit{Theme 4 -- Session Management.}
  1502→Nineteen respondents (38\%) commented on session management. The most common request (14 of 19) was a cross-session history so developers could compare token usage across working days. Seven participants found \texttt{/reset} confusing (``does it clear pins or just the turn counter?'') --- it resets only the turn counter, but the label needs clarifying. Four reported the duplicate-tab bug in multi-root workspaces, and six reported the pin/unpin reversion bug; both were fixed in v3.1.2. Eleven respondents appreciated the startup notification showing the previous session's peak usage.
  1503→
  1504→\textit{Theme 5 -- Compaction Workflow.}
  1505→Twenty-four respondents (48\%) used \texttt{/compaction}. Sixteen of them (67\%) said the per-turn growth projection helped them spot the compaction point --- the turn at which context would exceed the rot threshold --- before it happened. Eight asked for an automatic warning from \texttt{@tokalator} when per-turn growth crosses a set limit, similar to VS Code v1.110's automatic compaction. Four participants noted that the output did not separate file-token growth from conversation-history growth, making it hard to decide whether to close tabs or summarize the chat; this decomposition is planned.
  1506→
  1507→\subsection*{A.5.1 \quad Survey Questions (10 Items)}
  1508→
  1509→The survey comprised 10 items administered in person during the three community sessions
