\subsection{AI-assisted development sessions}


All sessions used GitHub Copilot (VS~Code) for inline code completion and Claude (Anthropic) for interactive pair-programming via chat. The AI tools accelerated implementation; all design decisions, architectural choices, and mathematical formulations were made by the authors. The cumulative Git statistics across all 102 commits are: \textit{+73,402} insertions and \textit{--18,909} deletions, resulting in a net codebase of approximately 54,000 lines (including content, configuration, and documentation). The current TypeScript/TSX codebase totals \textit{20,814~LOC} (Table~\ref{tab:codebase}), indicating that about 72\% of all code written was later refactored or replaced. This is a natural consequence of the iterative, AI-assisted development workflow, where rapid prototyping is followed by manual refinement.


\section{Survey Instrument and Aggregated Results}
\label{app:survey}

This appendix provides the full 10-item survey instrument used in Section~\ref{sec:survey}, the participant demographic profile (including gender distribution: 35~Male, 13~Female, 2~Non-binary or undisclosed), aggregated Likert-scale response distributions with bar charts, the five-theme qualitative summary, and representative verbatim quotes.

\subsection*{A.5.0 \quad Qualitative Theme Descriptions}

\textit{Theme 1 -- Preview and Turn Improvement.}
Forty-one of 50 respondents (82\%) named the \texttt{/preview} command as the most immediately useful feature. Representative quotes: ``I never realized a single turn could cost 6K tokens; the preview showed me my system prompt was the main culprit'' and ``I cut my average turn from 4,200 to 1,800 tokens after seeing the per-turn breakdown.'' Participants who ran \texttt{/preview} before sending proactively trimmed their context; those who skipped it could not estimate per-turn cost. Eleven participants (22\%) asked for a \emph{suggested turn structure} that would split large messages into smaller, cheaper exchanges --- noted as future work.

\textit{Theme 2 -- Tokenizer Accuracy.}
Twenty-six respondents (52\%) raised tokenizer accuracy concerns, mostly around non-English content. Eight developers working on Turkish or Arabic codebases found the Google heuristic ($\approx$4 chars/token) underestimated counts by 15--32\% compared to Google's \texttt{countTokens} API. Three respondents flagged that the \texttt{cl100k\_base} vocabulary was used for OpenAI models that had moved to \texttt{o200k\_base}; this was confirmed and corrected in v3.1.2. Five participants asked how Tokalator would handle future BPE vocabularies --- addressed in the Limitations section.

\textit{Theme 3 -- Model Synchronization.}
Thirty-two respondents (64\%) encountered the model-mismatch problem: their Copilot chat had switched to Claude Opus~4.6 while Tokalator's status bar still showed GPT~5.3 from a previous session, producing wrong budget figures. This was the most-requested fix and drove the auto-sync feature in v3.1.2, which reads \texttt{request.model} on every \texttt{@tokalator} command. After the fix was explained, 29 of the 32 (91\%) said it met their need; 3 asked for the sync to also update the web calculator automatically (planned).

\textit{Theme 4 -- Session Management.}
Nineteen respondents (38\%) commented on session management. The most common request (14 of 19) was a cross-session history so developers could compare token usage across working days. Seven participants found \texttt{/reset} confusing (``does it clear pins or just the turn counter?'') --- it resets only the turn counter, but the label needs clarifying. Four reported the duplicate-tab bug in multi-root workspaces, and six reported the pin/unpin reversion bug; both were fixed in v3.1.2. Eleven respondents appreciated the startup notification showing the previous session's peak usage.

\textit{Theme 5 -- Compaction Workflow.}
Twenty-four respondents (48\%) used \texttt{/compaction}. Sixteen of them (67\%) said the per-turn growth projection helped them spot the compaction point --- the turn at which context would exceed the rot threshold --- before it happened. Eight asked for an automatic warning from \texttt{@tokalator} when per-turn growth crosses a set limit, similar to VS Code v1.110's automatic compaction. Four participants noted that the output did not separate file-token growth from conversation-history growth, making it hard to decide whether to close tabs or summarize the chat; this decomposition is planned.

\subsection*{A.5.1 \quad Survey Questions (10 Items)}

The survey comprised 10 items administered in person during the three community sessions
\ifanonymous
