   381→  In v3.1.3 the Chat Participant reads \texttt{request.model} on every
   382→  \texttt{@tokalator} command and auto-syncs the context window, tokenizer,
   383→  and rot threshold to match the active Copilot model via
   384→  \texttt{findModel()}.}
   385→\label{fig:seq}
   386→\end{figure*}
   387→
   388→The central data structure is \texttt{ContextSnapshot}, emitted on every
   389→state change, containing: a timestamp, the active file, a list of all open
   390→tabs (each with URI, estimated tokens, relevance score, and diagnostics),
   391→the set of pinned files, total estimated tokens, window capacity, usage
   392→percentage, budget level (\texttt{low}/\texttt{medium}/\texttt{high}),
   393→context health status with reasons, model identifier, workspace-level
   394→file statistics, the tokenizer type in use, per-turn history, and a
   395→\texttt{BudgetBreakdown} struct itemising files, system prompt, instructions,
   396→conversation, and output reservation.
   397→
   398→
   399→\subsection{Software Functionalities}
   400→\label{sec:functionalities}
   401→
   402→\subsubsection{VS~Code Extension}
   403→
   404→The extension provides eight core functionalities.
   405→
   406→\textit{1. Real-time token budget monitoring.}
   407→The status bar displays a continuously updated summary (e.g.,
   408→\texttt{\$(check) 262K / 400K (65.5\%) -- GPT 5.4 Model}).
   409→A sidebar webview dashboard displays the full breakdown: budget level
   410→(low $<60\%$, medium $60$--$85\%$, or high $>85\%$), per-file token
   411→estimates, pinned file count, conversation turn count, and context health
   412→warnings.
   413→
   414→The total estimated tokens are computed as the sum of five components:
   415→\begin{equation}
   416→  T_{\text{total}} = T_{\text{files}} + T_{\text{sys}} + T_{\text{instr}}
   417→                   + T_{\text{conv}} + T_{\text{out}}
   418→\end{equation}
   419→where $T_{\text{files}} = \sum_i \text{tokens}(\text{tab}_i)$ is the sum
   420→of per-file BPE counts across all open tabs;
   421→$T_{\text{sys}} = 2{,}000$ is the estimated system prompt overhead;
   422→$T_{\text{instr}} = 500 \times n_{\text{instr}}$ accounts for instruction
   423→files detected in the workspace;
   424→$T_{\text{conv}} = 800 \times t$ estimates accumulated conversation
   425→history at turn~$t$; and
   426→$T_{\text{out}} = 4{,}000$ reserves tokens for the model's response.
   427→These overhead constants are empirically informed estimates of GitHub
   428→Copilot's context construction; the actual assistant context logic is
   429→proprietary (see Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).
   430→
   431→\textit{2. Tab relevance scoring.}
   432→Each open tab receives a relevance score $R \in [0, 1]$ computed as a
   433→weighted sum of five signals:
   434→\begin{equation}
   435→\begin{aligned}
   436→R =\;& 0.25\,S_{\text{lang}} + 0.30\,S_{\text{import}}
   437→     + 0.20\,S_{\text{path}} \\
   438→     &+ 0.15\,S_{\text{recency}} + 0.10\,S_{\text{diag}}
   439→\end{aligned}
   440→\end{equation}
   441→where $S_{\text{lang}} \in \{0, 1\}$ indicates language match with the
   442→active file;
   443→$S_{\text{import}} \in \{0, 1\}$ indicates an import relationship
   444→(detected via regex for TypeScript/JavaScript, Python, Go, Java, and a
   445→generic fallback);
   446→$S_{\text{path}} \in [0, 1]$ measures shared directory depth (ratio of
   447→shared path prefix segments to total depth);
   448→$S_{\text{recency}} \in \{0, 0.53, 1\}$ reflects edit recency (1.0 if
   449→edited within 2 minutes, 0.53 within 10 minutes, 0 otherwise); and
   450→$S_{\text{diag}} \in \{0, 1\}$ flags files with compiler diagnostics.
   451→Pinned and active files are overridden to $R = 1.0$.
   452→
   453→The weights reflect a deliberate engineering trade-off:
   454→import relationships ($w=0.30$) receive the highest weight because a
   455→file explicitly imported by the active file is almost certainly needed;
   456→language match ($w=0.25$) is the next strongest signal since cross-language
   457→files (\texttt{.json} configs alongside \texttt{.tsx} code) are common
   458→distractors;
   459→path similarity ($w=0.20$) captures co-location patterns;
   460→edit recency ($w=0.15$) reflects the developer's current working set; and
   461→diagnostics ($w=0.10$) provide a weak signal that files with errors are
   462→being actively debugged.
   463→The $S_{\text{recency}}$ intermediate value of $0.53 = \nicefrac{0.08}{0.15}$
   464→is the ratio of the partial recency credit to the full recency weight.
   465→All signals are syntactic and compute client-side with no model calls,
   466→completing in $<$5\,ms for 30+~open tabs --- a hard requirement for
   467→real-time IDE extensions where semantic approaches requiring embedding
   468→inference would exceed the interactive-latency budget.
   469→These weights are configurable and could benefit from empirical calibration
   470→in future work.
   471→
   472→Algorithm~\ref{alg:scoring} formalizes the scoring and optimization
   473→procedure.
   474→
   475→\begin{algorithm}[htp!]
   476→\DontPrintSemicolon
   477→\SetAlgoLined
   478→\KwIn{Open tabs $\mathcal{T} = \{t_1, \dots, t_n\}$, active file $f$,
   479→      threshold $\tau = 0.3$}
   480→\KwOut{Scored tabs with distractor labels; optimized tab set $\mathcal{T}'$}
   481→\BlankLine
   482→\ForEach{$t_i \in \mathcal{T}$}{
   483→  \If{$t_i$ is pinned \textbf{or} $t_i = f$}{
   484→    $R_i \gets 1.0$\;
   485→  }
   486→  \Else{
   487→    $S_{\text{lang}} \gets \mathbb{1}[\text{lang}(t_i) = \text{lang}(f)]$\;
   488→    $S_{\text{import}} \gets \mathbb{1}[\text{imports}(f) \ni t_i]$\;
   489→    $S_{\text{path}} \gets \text{sharedDepth}(t_i, f) \;/\; \text{totalDepth}(t_i)$\;
   490→    $S_{\text{recency}} \gets \begin{cases}
   491→      1.0 & \text{if edited} < 2\,\text{min ago} \\
   492→      0.53 & \text{if edited} < 10\,\text{min ago} \\
   493→      0 & \text{otherwise}\end{cases}$\;
   494→    $S_{\text{diag}} \gets \mathbb{1}[\text{diagnostics}(t_i) > 0]$\;
   495→    $R_i \gets 0.25 S_{\text{lang}} + 0.30 S_{\text{import}} +
   496→               0.20 S_{\text{path}} + 0.15 S_{\text{recency}} +
   497→               0.10 S_{\text{diag}}$\;
   498→  }
   499→  Label $t_i$ as \textit{distractor} if $R_i < \tau$\;
   500→}
   501→\BlankLine
   502→\tcp{Optimization: close distractors to free context budget}
   503→$\mathcal{D} \gets \{t_i \in \mathcal{T} \mid R_i < \tau\}$\;
   504→$\mathcal{T}' \gets \mathcal{T} \setminus \mathcal{D}$\;
   505→$\Delta T \gets \sum_{t_i \in \mathcal{D}} \text{tokens}(t_i)$\;
   506→\KwRet{$\mathcal{T}'$, scores $\{R_i\}$, freed tokens $\Delta T$}\;
   507→\caption{Tab Relevance Scoring and Context Optimization}
   508→\label{alg:scoring}
   509→\end{algorithm}
   510→
   511→\textit{3. Chat participant with 11 commands.}
   512→The \texttt{@tokalator} chat participant responds to:
   513→\texttt{/count} (budget status),
   514→\texttt{/breakdown} (per-file tokens),
   515→\texttt{/optimize} (close low-relevance tabs),
   516→\texttt{/pin}/\texttt{/unpin} (pin management),
   517→\texttt{/instructions} (scan instruction files and their token cost),
   518→\texttt{/model} (switch model profile),
   519→\texttt{/compaction} (per-turn growth analysis),
   520→\texttt{/preview} (preview next-turn token cost before sending),
   521→\texttt{/reset} and \texttt{/exit} (session management).
   522→Starting in v3.1.3, every \texttt{@tokalator} chat request reads
   523→\texttt{request.model} and automatically syncs the context window,
   524→tokenizer, and rot threshold to match the active Copilot model via
   525→\texttt{findModel()}, eliminating a source of confusion when users switch
   526→models in the Copilot UI without manually updating Tokalator.
   527→%% [U2] Model auto-sync added in v3.1.3
   528→
   529→\textit{4. Context optimization.}
   530→The \texttt{/optimize} command identifies open tabs with $R < 0.3$ and
   531→closes them to free up context budget.
   532→The Dashboard also provides an \textit{Optimize Tabs} button triggering the
   533→same behaviour.
   534→This reduces attention dilution by removing files unlikely to be relevant
   535→to the current coding task.
   536→
   537→\textit{5. Session persistence.}
   538→Session summaries (peak tokens, turns, model, top edited files) are saved to
   539→\texttt{workspaceState} on exit and shown as a notification on the next
   540→activation.
   541→Pinned files and model selection persist across VS~Code restarts.
   542→
   543→\textit{6. Instruction file scanner.}
   544→The extension detects and tokenizes instruction files that AI coding
   545→assistants automatically inject into every prompt.
   546→It searches nine patterns covering all major coding assistant ecosystems:
   547→\texttt{.github/copilot-instructions.md},
   548→\texttt{CLAUDE.md}, \texttt{AGENTS.md},
   549→\texttt{.cursorrules}, \texttt{.instructions.md},
   550→\texttt{.github/instructions/}, \texttt{.claude/*.md},
   551→\texttt{.copilot/skills/}, and \texttt{.github/skills/}.
   552→This reveals the hidden token cost of instruction files that are otherwise
   553→invisible to the developer.
   554→
   555→\textit{7. Session logger.}
   556→Opt-in anonymized research logging records aggregate context metrics
   557→(token counts, budget levels, provider distribution) per session without
   558→capturing filenames or code content.
   559→Version~3.1.3 resolved four critical bugs identified in the field:
   560→(a)~stale token counts when \texttt{isRefreshing} was \texttt{true} were
   561→fixed via a \texttt{pendingRefresh} flag that queues a follow-up refresh;
   562→(b)~duplicate tab entries in multi-root workspaces were fixed by introducing
   563→a \texttt{seenUris} \texttt{Set} and matching workspace folders by path;
   564→(c)~pin-state reversion (pinned files reverted to scored mode after tab
   565→switching) was fixed by persisting the pin set on every mutation;
   566→and (d)~model auto-sync ensures the tokenizer always matches the active
   567→Copilot model~\citep{vscode2026v110}.
   568→%% [U3] 4 critical bugs fixed in v3.1.3
   569→
   570→\textit{8. 17 model profiles (single source of truth).}
   571→Model profiles for 6~Anthropic, 7~OpenAI, and 4~Google models are defined
   572→in a single \texttt{models.json} file; the TypeScript module
   573→(\texttt{modelProfiles.ts}) is regenerated via
   574→\texttt{npm run generate-models} to eliminate manual duplication.
   575→Each profile stores the model identifier, display label, provider, context
   576→window size, maximum output tokens, and the provider-specific rot threshold
   577→(the number of conversation turns after which context rot risk rises).
   578→%% [I1/U1] 17 model profiles with codegen, single source of truth
   579→
   580→
   581→\subsubsection{Web Platform}
   582→
   583→The web platform at \url{https://tokalator.wiki} includes nine calculators:
   584→
   585→\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
   586→  \item \textit{Cost Calculator}: Token cost calculation with Cobb--Douglas
   587→    quality modelling for Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google models.
   588→    Supports tiered pricing detection: when the total prompt length exceeds
   589→    Anthropic's 200\,K-token threshold, \emph{all} input tokens are billed
   590→    at the extended rate ($2\times$ standard input cost), not only those
   591→    above the threshold.
   592→
   593→  \item \textit{Context Optimizer}: Visualizes context window allocation
   594→    across system prompt, user input, reserved output, and free space.
   595→    Computes usage percentage and generates warnings.
   596→
   597→  \item \textit{Model Comparison}: Cross-provider cost and capability
   598→    comparison across all 17 models.
